   "text": {
    "L1": "Science is a shared human effort. People across times and cultures add to knowledge.",
    "L2": "A discovery is not usually made by one genius alone. Others observe, test and build on earlier work.",
    "L3": "Aryabhata studied astronomy, C.V. Raman studied scattered light, and ISRO teams extend knowledge through shared work.",
    "L4": "Scientists share methods and results so others can check, repeat and improve them.",
    "L5": "Knowledge about malaria grew step by step, from observations near swamps to studies of parasites and mosquitoes.",
    "L6": "In 1880, Laveran saw parasites in blood. In 1897, Ronald Ross found the parasite inside a mosquito.",
    "L7": "Other scientists identified Anopheles mosquitoes, and health teams used this knowledge to fight malaria.",
    "L8": "Anyone can contribute by observing carefully, asking questions and recording evidence. Now choose the practice that makes results checkable."
   },
